{"chunk_id": "5ba468068c76", "text": "After the defeat of Ravana, crowds of monkeys and titans gather as Sita is brought before Rama. When attendants attempt to clear the onlookers, Rama rebukes them, insisting the people should witness Sita beside him, declaring that a woman\u2019s true protection lies in her conduct, not seclusion. Sita approaches with shy devotion, radiant at seeing her husband again after long separation. Yet Rama, torn by concern for public opinion, delivers a shocking speech. He declares that he fought the war not solely for her but to avenge his honor and the insult to his lineage. Suspecting her purity after her captivity in Ravana\u2019s house, he tells her she is free to go wherever she wishes. The moment marks one of the Ramayana\u2019s most painful turning points, where duty, reputation, and personal love violently collide.\nAfter rescuing Sita, Rama publicly declares he fought to defend honor, not for her, and rejects her due to suspicion about her purity.\nThemes: honor and reputation, marital fidelity, public duty vs personal love, humiliation, moral conflict", "vector": [-0.004461226519197226, -0.033710017800331116, -0.019904963672161102, 0.006223645061254501, -0.006748692132532597, 0.0020784500520676374, -0.014647805131971836, 0.009337140247225761, -0.023984944447875023, -0.020868105813860893, 0.015356785617768764, 0.012146308086812496, 0.014179610647261143, -0.00015404440637212247, -0.008173341862857342, -0.005063191056251526, 0.04936109855771065, -0.007497803308069706, 0.013497384265065193, -0.027797387912869453, -0.004819061141461134, -0.017563989385962486, -0.012032603845000267, 0.007698458153754473, -0.030419277027249336, 0.0038191310595721006, 0.03512797877192497, -0.0404520221054554, 0.02347661927342415, -0.03084734082221985, -0.023663897067308426, -0.021389808505773544, -0.009925727732479572, -0.01557081751525402, -0.041655950248241425, -0.002752315951511264, -0.006394201889634132, -0.013310106471180916, 0.005641745869070291